# made = Object.create(above, { constructor: { value: named } }); made.constructor()
# Object.defineProperties(defined, { valueOf: { value: named } }); defined.valueOf()
# The map is an object literal that goes nowhere but into the call: a name that it was written with is a property of its own, so what Object.prototype holds under that name does not answer for it.
# Taken for a descriptor, the built-in `Object` gives what it holds under `value`: undefined (primitive 0) here, in a program a value that nobody knows. What the reads find beside `named` stands above their receivers.
# RUN: valueflow-why values=f0:v12 values=f0:v22
# CHECK: values=f0:v12
# CHECK: operand 0 {{*}} 2 members: Object f1
# CHECK: values=f0:v22
# CHECK: operand 0 {{*}} 2 members: ObjectPrototypeValueOf f1

